{"headers":{"content-type":"text/event-stream; charset=utf-8","cache-control":"no-cache"},"model":"claude-opus-5","prompt":"user: <system-reminder>\nAs you answer the user's questions, you can use the following context:\n# currentDate\nToday's date is 2026-08-28.\n\n      IMPORTANT: this context may or may not be relevant to your tasks. You should not respond to this context unless it is highly relevant to your task.\n</system-reminder>\n\n\nmówimy po polsku\nsystem: Available agent types for the Agent tool:\n- claude: Catch-all for any task that doesn't fit a more specific agent. FleetView's default when no agent name is typed. (Tools: *)\n- claude-code-guide: Use this agent when the user asks questions (\"Can Claude...\", \"Does Claude...\", \"How do I...\") about: (1) Claude Code (the CLI tool) - features, hooks, slash commands, MCP servers, settings, IDE integrations, keyboard shortcuts; (2) Claude Agent SDK - building custom agents; (3) Claude API (formerly Anthropic API) - Messages API for directly passing messages to Claude, Tool Runner (`client.beta.messages.tool_runner`) for running an agentic loop over your own tools, manual tool-use loops, Managed Agents for server-hosted agents with a managed sandbox, prompt caching, and general Anthropic SDK usage; (4) Claude Tag (Claude in Slack) - what it is, setting it up for a Slack workspace, `/install-slack-app`; (5) `claude plugin eval` (writing and running plugin eval suites, its JSON/report, sandbox, CI, early-access enablement) and the `/skill-doctor` report. **IMPORTANT:** Before spawning a new agent, check if there is already a running or recently completed claude-code-guide agent that you can continue via SendMessage. (Tools: Bash, Read, WebFetch, WebSearch)\n- Explore: Read-only search agent for broad fan-out searches — when answering means sweeping many files, directories, or naming conventions and you only need the conclusion, not the file dumps. It reads excerpts rather than whole files, so it locates code; it doesn't review or audit it. Specify search breadth: \"medium\" for moderate exploration, \"very thorough\" for multiple locations and naming conventions. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)\n- general-purpose: General-purpose agent for researching complex questions, searching for code, and executing multi-step tasks. When you are searching for a keyword or file and are not confident that you will find the right match in the first few tries use this agent to perform the search for you. (Tools: *)\n- Plan: Software architect agent for designing implementation plans. Use this when you need to plan the implementation strategy for a task. Returns step-by-step plans, identifies critical files, and considers architectural trade-offs. (Tools: All tools except Agent, Artifact, ExitPlanMode, Edit, Write, NotebookEdit)\n- statusline-setup: Use this agent to configure the user's Claude Code status line setting. (Tools: Read, Edit)\n\nWhen you launch multiple agents for independent work, send them in a single message with multiple tool uses so they run concurrently.\n\nThe following skills are available for use with the Skill tool:\n\n- 101-agents-md: Create or surgically update a repo's agent onboarding doc (AGENTS.md, \"Repository Guidelines\"). Use when the user asks to \"create AGENTS.md\", wants an \"agent onboarding doc\", a \"contributor guide for agents\", or a refresh of an existing agent guide.\n- 101-archive: Close a completed change — stamp change.md as archived, move the folder into context/archive/, commit. Use AFTER /101-impl-review. Trigger phrases -- \"archive the change\", \"close this change\", \"zamknij zmianę\".\n- 101-bootstrapper: Scaffold a project into the current directory from the context/foundation/tech-stack.md hand-off, always preserving context/. Use when the user says \"bootstrap the project\", \"scaffold the app\", \"postaw projekt\". Use AFTER /101-tech-stack-selector.\n- 101-e2e: Add real-browser tests to a change — ONE verified test per risk, never a surface sweep. Drives a plan's browser-level phases or a single named risk; needs the feature built. Trigger phrases -- \"e2e\", \"browser test\", \"Playwright test\", \"test przeglądarkowy\".\n- 101-frame: Challenge framing assumptions about WHAT the problem is before planning HOW — when an observation and its stated cause arrive bundled, or scope/design is questioned. Triggers -- \"fix\", \"bug\", \"root cause\", \"should we even\", \"is this the right\", \"rethink\", \"przemyśl zakres\". Use BEFORE /101-plan.\n- 101-goal-implement: Autonomously execute an approved plan from context/changes/<change-id>/plan.md with zero human interaction — the unattended sibling of /101-implement for goal-mode and headless (claude -p) runs. Trigger phrases -- \"run the plan unattended\", \"autonomous implementation\", \"headless\".\n- 101-health-check: Operational readiness audit of an existing project — dependencies, tests, CI, config; writes context/foundation/health-check.md with an impact-ordered fix list. Trigger phrases -- \"health check\", \"audit my project\", \"audyt projektu\". Use AFTER /101-stack-assess, BEFORE /101-agents-md.\n- 101-impl-review: Review the implementation against its plan — drift, risky decisions, pattern breaks — then triage each finding interactively. Use AFTER /101-implement (or /101-tdd, /101-e2e), BEFORE /101-archive. Invoke as /101-impl-review <change-id|plan-path> [phase N] for a fresh run, or with a saved-review path to resume triage. Trigger phrases -- \"review the implementation\", \"impl review\", \"oceń implementację\".\n- 101-impl-review-ci: Run the implementation-vs-plan review non-interactively on a PR in CI — the pipeline sibling of /101-impl-review; commits the report and posts line + summary comments. Use when the request mentions CI, GitHub Actions, automated PR review, or reviewing a PR without a human in the loop.\n- 101-implement: Execute an approved plan from context/changes/<change-id>/plan.md phase by phase, with a confirmation gate and a commit per phase. Use when the user says \"implement the plan\", \"execute the plan\", \"wdróż plan\", or names a change to implement. Use AFTER /101-plan.\n- 101-infra-research: Recommend a deployment platform via a constraint interview plus bias-checked web research; writes context/foundation/infrastructure.md with a scored comparison. Trigger phrases -- \"where should I deploy\", \"infra research\", \"wybierz platformę\". Use AFTER /101-prd or /101-tech-stack-selector.\n- 101-init: Scaffold the context directory skeleton — context/{changes,archive,foundation}/ with convention READMEs — creating only what is missing. Trigger phrases -- \"init the context\", \"set up the project skeleton\", \"zainicjuj kontekst\". Optional; run once, BEFORE /101-shape or /101-new.\n- 101-lesson: Append ONE recurring rule to context/foundation/lessons.md — the learning loop the toolkit reads at start. Trigger phrases -- \"save a lesson\", \"add a recurring rule\", \"zapisz lekcję\".\n- 101-mom-test: Check whether a problem is real BEFORE anyone builds a solution — non-leading critique, interview script, and go/no-go thresholds grounded in past behavior. Trigger phrases -- \"mom test\", \"is this problem real\", \"walidacja problemu\". Use AFTER /101-opportunity-map, BEFORE /101-shape.\n- 101-new: Open a new change folder under context/changes/<change-id> with a change.md identity file; all work on the change lives there until archived. Trigger phrases -- \"new change\", \"open a change\", \"nowa zmiana\".\n- 101-opportunity-map: Turn recurring frictions or unmet needs into a build / buy / complement / wait decision artifact with at most one recommended candidate. Use at the very start, before shaping or validation (\"is this worth building\", \"mapa okazji\"). Use BEFORE /101-mom-test and /101-shape.\n- 101-pack-init: Package a custom AI toolkit (skills + rules + installer) as a versioned npm package skeleton — a distribution utility OUTSIDE the SDLC core. Trigger phrases -- \"package my toolkit\", \"make my skills installable\", \"spakuj mój toolkit\". First link of the trio — pack-init → setup-cicd → tf-registry.\n- 101-plan: Turn a task plus existing frame/research artifacts into a complete implementation plan with NO open questions at context/changes/<change-id>/plan.md. Trigger phrases -- \"plan this change\", \"create implementation plan\", \"zaplanuj zmianę\". Use AFTER /101-new, BEFORE /101-implement.\n- 101-plan-review: Review an implementation plan for substance, feasibility, and architectural fitness BEFORE any code exists; produces a 5-dimension scorecard with triage. Trigger phrases -- \"review this plan\", \"is this plan good\", \"oceń plan\". Use AFTER /101-plan, BEFORE /101-implement.\n- 101-prd: Generate context/foundation/prd.md from shape-notes (or raw notes) against the locked PRD schema; auto-routes greenfield vs brownfield. Use AFTER /101-shape, not in place of it. Trigger phrases -- \"write the PRD\", \"generate PRD\", \"stwórz PRD\".\n- 101-research: Answer a research question about the existing system before planning; produces context/changes/<change-id>/research.md with code references. Trigger phrases -- \"research the codebase\", \"how does X work here\", \"zbadaj kod\". Use BEFORE /101-plan when the system is unfamiliar.\n- 101-roadmap: Generate context/foundation/roadmap.md from a PRD as ordered vertical end-to-end slices — user-visible milestones. Use AFTER /101-prd; NOT for per-change planning (that's /101-plan). Trigger phrases -- \"generate roadmap\", \"what should I build first\", \"stwórz roadmapę\".\n- 101-rule-review: Review the condition of an AI rules file (AGENTS.md, CLAUDE.md, .cursor/rules/*.mdc, any target tool); produces a five-check scorecard with concrete fixes — grades the artifact, never the decisions in it. Trigger phrases -- \"review AI rules\", \"audit AGENTS.md\", \"check my CLAUDE.md\".\n- 101-setup-cicd: Generate the validation + publish pipeline for a packaged AI toolkit — a distribution utility OUTSIDE the SDLC core. Trigger phrases -- \"publish pipeline for the toolkit\", \"CI for my toolkit package\", \"potok publikacji\". Second link of the trio — pack-init → setup-cicd → tf-registry.\n- 101-shape: Facilitate a structured discovery conversation turning an idea — greenfield or brownfield, auto-detected — into context/foundation/shape-notes.md. Use BEFORE /101-prd, not in place of it. Trigger phrases -- \"new project\", \"I have an idea\", \"od pomysłu\", \"greenfield\", \"brownfield\".\n- 101-stack-assess: Assess an existing project's stack for agent-friendliness against the four quality gates; writes context/foundation/stack-assessment.md. Trigger phrases -- \"assess my stack\", \"is my stack agent-friendly\", \"oceń mój stack\". Use AFTER /101-prd (brownfield), BEFORE /101-health-check.\n- 101-tdd: Drive an approved plan's TDD-able phases test-first through red→green→refactor; everything else routes to /101-implement. Use when the user says \"tdd\", \"test-first\", \"red green refactor\", or wants a plan phase driven by tests.\n- 101-tech-stack-selector: Pick a starter and stack for a greenfield project after the PRD is written — reads context/foundation/prd.md, writes the context/foundation/tech-stack.md hand-off. Trigger phrases -- \"what stack should I use\", \"pick a stack\", \"co wybrać do projektu\". Use AFTER /101-prd, BEFORE /101-bootstrapper.\n- 101-test-plan: Stateful, phased test-rollout orchestrator for existing products — writes context/foundation/test-plan.md, then drives each phase through the change lifecycle. Trigger phrases -- \"create test plan\", \"test strategy\", \"strategia testów\". Use AFTER /101-prd and /101-roadmap. Brownfield only.\n- 101-tf-registry: Generate Terraform for a private npm registry (AWS CodeArtifact-shaped) hosting a packaged AI toolkit — a distribution utility OUTSIDE the SDLC core, optional last link of the trio (pack-init → setup-cicd → tf-registry). Trigger phrases -- \"private npm registry terraform\", \"prywatny rejestr\".\n- dataviz: Use this skill whenever you are about to create ANY chart, graph, plot, dashboard, or data visualization, in ANY output medium — an HTML or React artifact, inline SVG, plotting code in any library (matplotlib, plotly, d3, Recharts, …), an image/PNG you will render and upload, or a chart shared into Slack. Read it BEFORE writing the first line of chart code, choosing chart colors, building a stat tile / meter / KPI row, or laying out a dashboard. Produces visualizations that read as one system — elegant, accessible, consistent in light and dark — using a brand-neutral placeholder palette you swap for your own. Teaches a design-system-agnostic method: a form heuristic, a color formula with a runnable validator, mark specs, and interaction rules. A validated default palette is documented in `references/palette.md` — swap that file's values for your brand's. Triggers on: \"chart\", \"graph\", \"plot\", \"data viz\", \"visualization\", \"dashboard\", \"analytics\", \"visualize data\", \"categorical colors\", \"sequential / diverging palette\", \"stat tile\", \"sparkline\", \"heatmap\", \"legend\", \"axis\", \"tooltip\", \"chart colors\", \"color by series\".\n- artifact-design: Design guidance and fundamentals for Artifacts. - Load before writing any artifact, including a skill-instructed Markdown one - Markdown is never a shortcut past the design pass.\n- artifact-diagramming: Diagramming know-how for Artifacts - when a picture earns its place, how to draw one that shows the real mechanism, and the inline-SVG mechanics that keep it legible in both themes.\n- update-config: Use this skill to configure the Claude Code harness via settings.json. Automated behaviors (\"from now on when X\", \"each time X\", \"whenever X\", \"before/after X\") require hooks configured in settings.json - the harness executes these, not Claude, so memory/preferences cannot fulfill them. Also use for: permissions (\"allow X\", \"add permission\", \"move permission to\"), env vars (\"set X=Y\"), hook troubleshooting, or any changes to settings.json/settings.local.json files. Examples: \"allow npm commands\", \"add bq permission to global settings\", \"move permission to user settings\", \"set DEBUG=true\", \"when claude stops show X\". For simple settings like theme/model, suggest the /config command.\n- keybindings-help: Use when the user wants to customize keyboard shortcuts, rebind keys, add chord bindings, or modify ~/.claude/keybindings.json. Examples: \"rebind ctrl+s\", \"add a chord shortcut\", \"change the submit key\", \"customize keybindings\".\n- code-review: Review the current diff, or a PR number/branch/path target, for correctness bugs and reuse/simplification/efficiency cleanups at the given effort level (low/medium: fewer, high-confidence findings; high→max: broader coverage, may include uncertain findings); with no level given, it reuses the level you typed last. Pass --comment to post findings as inline PR comments, or --fix to apply the findings to the working tree after the review.\n- simplify: Review the changed code for reuse, simplification, efficiency, and altitude cleanups, then apply the fixes. Quality only — it does not hunt for bugs; use /code-review for that.\n- fewer-permission-prompts: Scan your transcripts for common read-only Bash and MCP tool calls, then add a prioritized allowlist to project .claude/settings.json to reduce permission prompts.\n- loop: Run a prompt or slash command on a recurring interval (e.g. /loop 5m /foo). Omit the interval to let the model self-pace. - When the user wants to set up a recurring task, poll for status, or run something repeatedly on an interval (e.g. \"check the deploy every 5 minutes\", \"keep running /babysit-prs\"). Do NOT invoke for one-off tasks.\n- claude-api: Reference for the Claude API / Anthropic SDK — model ids, pricing, params, streaming, tool use, MCP, agents, caching, token counting, model migration.\nTRIGGER — read BEFORE opening the target file; don't skip because it \"looks like a one-liner\" — whenever: the prompt names Claude/Anthropic in any form (Claude, Anthropic, Fable, Opus, Sonnet, Haiku, `anthropic`, `@anthropic-ai`, `claude-*`, `us.anthropic.*`, `[1m]`); the user asks about an LLM (pricing/model choice/limits/caching) — never answer from memory; OR the task is LLM-shaped with provider unstated (agent/MCP/tool-definition/multi-agent/RAG/LLM-judge/computer-use; generate/summarize/extract/classify/rewrite/converse over NL; debugging refusals/cutoffs/streaming/tool-calls/tokens).\nSKIP only when another provider is being worked on (overrides all triggers): OpenAI/GPT/Gemini/Llama/Mistral/Cohere/Ollama named in the query; OR `grep -rE 'openai|langchain_openai|google.generativeai|genai|mistralai|cohere|ollama'` over the project hits (run this grep FIRST if no provider named — don't Read the file).\n- workflow-authoring: Reference for writing a Workflow tool script (script API and gotchas, resume, quality patterns, worked examples). Load before authoring a script for a workflow the user already opted into; it does not itself authorize running one.\n- run: Launch and drive this project's app to see a change working. Use when asked to run, start, or screenshot the app, or to confirm a change works in the real app (not just tests). First looks for a project skill that already covers launching the app; otherwise falls back to built-in patterns per project type (CLI, server, TUI, Electron, browser-driven, library).\n- init: Initialize a new CLAUDE.md file with codebase documentation\n- security-review: Complete a security review of the pending changes on the current branch\nassistant: Jasne, mówimy po polsku. W czym mogę pomóc?\nuser: <command-message>101-init</command-message>\n<command-name>/101-init</command-name>\n\nBase directory for this skill: /course/.claude/skills/101-init\n\n# 101-init — Context directory scaffold\n\nCreates the directories where the rest of the 101 set stores its artifacts,\nplus the load-bearing-names registry. Autonomy: writes files without asking,\nbut ONLY files that do not exist yet.\n\nInvocation: `/101-init` — no arguments; everything the run needs comes\nfrom the repository state.\n\n**This is NOT a precondition.** Every other 101 skill bootstraps its own\nfiles when it needs them. This skill exists purely for people who prefer to\nlay out the skeleton up front. Say this out loud when the user seems to\nbelieve running it is mandatory — the belief grows on its own otherwise.\n\n## The seven entities\n\nEach entity is handled **independently** — per-artifact idempotency, not\nall-or-nothing. A partially prepared repository is completed without\ntouching what already exists; a fully prepared one gets nothing but the\nstate printout.\n\n1. `context/changes/` — in-flight units of work, one folder per change.\n2. `context/archive/` — completed changes, moved here when archived.\n3. `context/foundation/` — living cross-change documents.\n4. `context/changes/README.md` — convention file (content below).\n5. `context/archive/README.md` — convention file (content below).\n6. `context/foundation/README.md` — convention file (content below).\n7. `docs/reference/contract-surfaces.md` — registry of load-bearing names\n   (create `docs/reference/` on the way if needed).\n\n**Never overwrite existing content.** An existing README or registry stays\nuntouched even when its content differs from the canonical text below —\nsomeone may have adapted it deliberately, and this skill has no authority\nto revert that.\n\n## README contents (write only when the file is absent)\n\n`context/changes/README.md` — say that this holds in-flight changes, one\nfolder per change at `context/changes/<change-id>/`, identified by a\n`change.md` identity file and opened with `/101-new`; that a change folder\ngathers everything change-scoped (research, frame, plan, reviews); and that\na finished change is moved under `context/archive/` with `/101-archive`.\n\n`context/archive/README.md` — say that this holds completed changes moved\nfrom `context/changes/` by `/101-archive`; that it is read-only by\nconvention; and that 101 skills refuse to write here (G-14).\n\n`context/foundation/README.md` — say that this holds living documents that\nspan multiple changes (each project picks which it needs — requirements,\ntech stack, roadmap, lessons, test plan), owned by the skills that read and\nwrite them, plus three conventions:\n- **Edit-in-place** (G-05a): when a foundation doc changes incrementally,\n  edit the existing file — never create dated just-in-case copies.\n- **Archive only when superseded**: a doc replaced by a new approach (not\n  merely refined) moves to `foundation/archive/YYYY-MM-DD-<doc>.md` and the\n  replacement takes the original path; nothing reads the archive routinely.\n- **Named anti-pattern — change-scoped doc in foundation**: anything tied\n  to a single change (its plan, research, review) belongs under\n  `context/changes/<change-id>/`, never here. Foundation is for what\n  outlives any one change.\n\n## Contract-surfaces registry (write only when absent)\n\n`docs/reference/contract-surfaces.md` starts as an **empty registry** of\nload-bearing names — the identifiers other code, tools or people depend on\nstaying stable. Give it a short intro explaining:\n- what belongs here: data **structures**, **routes/endpoints**, file and\n  message **formats**, **events** — one `##` heading per surface, with a\n  line or two on who depends on it;\n- how it is consumed: `/101-plan-review` pattern-scans plan text against\n  this file's H2 headings and flags plans that touch a registered surface\n  without acknowledging it. When the file is absent, that check is\n  **silently skipped** — so an empty-but-present registry is already\n  useful the day the first surface is added;\n- who owns what: **this skill owns the scaffold** — file creation and the\n  header/intro; **the user owns the entries** (any skill may add one, but\n  only on the user's request); **`/101-plan-review` is a read-only\n  consumer** and never writes here.\n\n## Ownership boundary — files this skill does NOT create\n\nDocuments with a dedicated owner are bootstrapped by that owner on first\nuse, never here. In particular:\n- `context/foundation/lessons.md` → owned by `/101-lesson`;\n- the starter registry → maintained with the toolkit\n  (`101-tech-stack-selector/references/`).\nThis keeps the responsibility line clean: 101-init owns the skeleton,\nowners own their documents.\n\nThe one deliberate exception is `docs/reference/contract-surfaces.md` —\nand it is an exception in appearance only: 101-init owns that file's\nscaffold (creation plus header), the user owns its entries, and\n`/101-plan-review` merely reads it. Scaffolding it here therefore creates\nnothing another skill owns.\n\n## State printout (fixed form)\n\nAfter processing all seven entities, print exactly one line per entity in\nthe order above, each marked **created** or **existed**, followed by a\nshort recap of what each of the three directories is for. Same form every\nrun — a re-run on a complete skeleton produces only\nthis printout. Mirror the user's language in the printout (G-11); keep the\nentity numbering internal (G-10).\n\n## Closing\n\nInit is a standalone convenience, not the first link of a mandatory\nchain — so the closing does NOT push a next command. State briefly what\nexists now: the three directories, their READMEs, the registry — each\ncreated or already present. Only when the user has already said they are\nstarting a project, add a single pointer to `/101-shape` as the natural\nnext step. Otherwise recommend nothing; never run anything yourself.\n\n## Invariants\n\n- Never overwrites existing content, even content that differs from\n  canonical.\n- Each of the seven entities is created independently; partial state is\n  completed without touching what exists.\n- Not a precondition for anything — other skills self-bootstrap; declare\n  this when relevant.\n- Never creates documents owned by other skills. The contract-surfaces\n  registry is not a violation of this: 101-init owns its scaffold, the\n  user owns its entries, `/101-plan-review` only reads it.\n- Never auto-runs a follow-up skill, and never pushes a command chain at\n  close.\n\n## Thresholds\n\n- Seven independent entities (three directories, three READMEs, one\n  registry).\n\n## Touchpoints\n\n- Creates the space every other 101 skill writes into.\n- `/101-shape` checks for `context/foundation/` and may offer to delegate\n  here when it is missing.\n- `/101-new` requires `context/changes/` and points here when absent.\n- `/101-plan-review` reads `docs/reference/contract-surfaces.md` when\n  present; its absence silently disables that one check.\n\nassistant: I'll check the current state of the repository first.\nBash({\"command\":\"ls -la /course/ 2>/dev/null && echo \\\"---CONTEXT---\\\" && ls -la /course/context/ 2>/dev/null; echo \\\"---DOCS---\\\" && ls -la /course/docs/reference/ 2>/dev/null\",\"description\":\"Inspect repo skeleton state\"})\nuser: Exit code 2\ntotal 12\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 root root 4096 Aug 28 11:51 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 1 root root 4096 Aug 28 11:40 ..\ndrwxr-xr-x 4 root root 4096 Aug 28 11:40 .claude\n---CONTEXT---\n---DOCS---\nassistant: \nWrite({\"file_path\":\"/course/context/changes/README.md\",\"content\":\"# context/changes/\\n\\nKatalog zmian w toku (in-flight). Jedna zmiana = jeden folder:\\n`context/changes/<change-id>/`.\\n\\n- Każdy folder zmiany jest identyfikowany przez plik tożsamości `change.md`.\\n- Nową zmianę otwiera się komendą `/101-new` — ona zakłada folder i `change.md`.\\n- Folder zmiany zbiera **wszystko, co jest zakresu tej zmiany**: research\\n  (`research.md`), framing (`frame.md`), plan (`plan.md`), recenzje planu\\n  i implementacji, notatki testowe.\\n- Zakończoną zmianę przenosi się do `context/archive/` komendą `/101-archive`.\\n\\nCokolwiek żyje dłużej niż jedna zmiana (wymagania, stack, roadmapa, lekcje)\\nnie należy tutaj — to `context/foundation/`.\\n\"})\nWrite({\"file_path\":\"/course/context/archive/README.md\",\"content\":\"# context/archive/\\n\\nKatalog zmian zakończonych. Foldery trafiają tu z `context/changes/` —\\nprzenosi je `/101-archive`, po ostemplowaniu `change.md` jako zarchiwizowany.\\n\\n- Katalog jest **tylko do odczytu z konwencji**: to zapis historyczny tego,\\n  co i dlaczego zostało zrobione.\\n- Skille 101 odmawiają zapisu w tym miejscu (G-14). Jeśli coś wymaga zmiany,\\n  robi się to w nowej zmianie w `context/changes/`, nie przez edycję archiwum.\\n\"})\nWrite({\"file_path\":\"/course/context/foundation/README.md\",\"content\":\"# context/foundation/\\n\\nDokumenty żywe, które **przekraczają pojedynczą zmianę**. Każdy projekt\\nwybiera te, których faktycznie potrzebuje — typowo: wymagania (`prd.md`),\\nnotatki z kształtowania (`shape-notes.md`), stack (`tech-stack.md`),\\nroadmapa (`roadmap.md`), lekcje (`lessons.md`), plan testów (`test-plan.md`),\\naudyty (`stack-assessment.md`, `health-check.md`), infrastruktura\\n(`infrastructure.md`).\\n\\nKażdy dokument ma swojego właściciela — skill, który go czyta i zapisuje.\\nDokumenty powstają dopiero wtedy, gdy właściciel ich potrzebuje; nic nie\\ntrzeba tworzyć na zapas.\\n\\n## Konwencje\\n\\n**Edycja w miejscu (G-05a).** Gdy dokument foundation zmienia się\\nprzyrostowo, edytujesz istniejący plik. Żadnych datowanych kopii „na wszelki\\nwypadek\\\" obok oryginału.\\n\\n**Archiwizacja tylko przy zastąpieniu.** Dokument, który został *zastąpiony*\\nnowym podejściem (a nie jedynie doprecyzowany), przenosi się do\\n`foundation/archive/YYYY-MM-DD-<doc>.md`, a następca zajmuje oryginalną\\nścieżkę. Archiwum nie jest czytane rutynowo — służy tylko do odtworzenia\\nhistorii decyzji.\\n\\n**Antywzorzec — dokument zakresu jednej zmiany w foundation.** Wszystko, co\\njest przywiązane do jednej zmiany (jej plan, research, recenzja), należy do\\n`context/changes/<change-id>/`, nigdy tutaj. Foundation jest dla tego, co\\nprzeżywa dowolną pojedynczą zmianę.\\n\"})\nWrite({\"file_path\":\"/course/docs/reference/contract-surfaces.md\",\"content\":\"# Contract surfaces — rejestr nazw nośnych\\n\\nRejestr identyfikatorów, na których stabilność liczy inny kod, narzędzia lub\\nludzie. Na razie **pusty** — to normalny stan startowy.\\n\\n## Co tu trafia\\n\\nJeden nagłówek `##` na powierzchnię kontraktu, a pod nim linijka–dwie o tym,\\nkto od niej zależy:\\n\\n- **struktury danych** — kształty rekordów, schematy, nazwy pól przekraczające\\n  granicę modułu;\\n- **trasy / endpointy** — ścieżki HTTP, nazwy komend CLI, sygnatury RPC;\\n- **formaty plików i wiadomości** — układ plików konfiguracyjnych, payloady\\n  kolejek, format eksportu;\\n- **zdarzenia** — nazwy i payloady eventów, na które ktoś nasłuchuje.\\n\\nNie trafiają tu wewnętrzne nazwy, które można zmienić jednym refaktorem bez\\nuzgadniania z kimkolwiek.\\n\\n## Jak jest konsumowany\\n\\n`/101-plan-review` skanuje tekst planu pod kątem nagłówków H2 z tego pliku\\ni oznacza plany, które ruszają zarejestrowaną powierzchnię, nie przyznając\\nsię do tego. Gdy pliku nie ma, ta kontrola jest **po cichu pomijana** —\\ndlatego pusty, ale istniejący rejestr jest już użyteczny w dniu, w którym\\ndopisuje się pierwszą powierzchnię.\\n\\n## Własność\\n\\n- **`/101-init` jest właścicielem rusztowania** — utworzenia pliku oraz tego\\n  nagłówka i wstępu.\\n- **Użytkownik jest właścicielem wpisów.** Dowolny skill może dopisać\\n  powierzchnię, ale wyłącznie na prośbę użytkownika.\\n- **`/101-plan-review` jest konsumentem tylko do odczytu** — nigdy tu nie\\n  pisze.\\n\\n---\\n\\n<!-- Pierwszą powierzchnię dopisz poniżej jako nagłówek ## -->\\n\"})\nuser: File created successfully at: /course/context/changes/README.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)\nFile created successfully at: /course/context/archive/README.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)\nFile created successfully at: /course/context/foundation/README.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)\nFile created successfully at: /course/docs/reference/contract-surfaces.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)\nassistant: Szkielet gotowy.\n\n```\ncontext/changes/                        utworzony\ncontext/archive/                        utworzony\ncontext/foundation/                     utworzony\ncontext/changes/README.md               utworzony\ncontext/archive/README.md               utworzony\ncontext/foundation/README.md            utworzony\ndocs/reference/contract-surfaces.md     utworzony\n```\n\n**Do czego służą trzy katalogi:**\n\n- `context/changes/` — zmiany w toku, jeden folder na zmianę, każdy z plikiem tożsamości `change.md`; tam ląduje research, plan i recenzje danej zmiany.\n- `context/archive/` — zmiany zakończone, przeniesione tu z `changes/`; tylko do odczytu z konwencji.\n- `context/foundation/` — dokumenty żywe, przekraczające pojedynczą zmianę (wymagania, stack, roadmapa, lekcje); każdy ma skill-właściciela i powstaje dopiero, gdy ten go potrzebuje.\n\nPlus `docs/reference/contract-surfaces.md` — pusty rejestr nazw nośnych; sam plik wystarczy, żeby `/101-plan-review` włączył kontrolę powierzchni kontraktu, gdy dopiszesz pierwszy wpis.\n\nUwaga na marginesie: `/101-init` nie jest warunkiem wstępnym niczego. Pozostałe skille 101 zakładają sobie potrzebne pliki same — ten skill jest wygodą dla osób, które wolą mieć układ rozrysowany z góry.\nuser: <command-message>101-shape</command-message>\n<command-name>/101-shape</command-name>\n<command-args>aplikacja webowa oceniająca kotki. dwa kotki, jeden po lewej, jeden po prawej. kliknięcie jednego to preferencja (+1). po kliknięciu wyświetlają się dwa kolejne kotki. po 10 rundach wyniki (globalne hall of cat fame).</command-args>\n\nBase directory for this skill: /course/.claude/skills/101-shape\n\n# 101-shape — Discovery facilitation\n\nLeads a structured conversation from a raw idea to shape-notes that\n`/101-prd` can generate a requirements document from. Two modes: new\nproduct (greenfield) and change to an existing system (brownfield).\n\nAutonomy: facilitator with hard stops. **Never generates domain content\nfor the user** — the two hard rules below govern everything else.\n\nOutput contract (checkpoint block, section order, FR shape, gap marker):\n`references/prd-schema.md`. Read it at the start of every run — do not\nwork from memory.\n\n## Two hard rules\n\n1. **Facilitator, not author.** Never write content the user did not say.\n   The only exception is mechanical formatting: numbering, headings, the\n   frontmatter skeleton.\n2. **Zero stack pre-decisions.** Never ask about frameworks, databases,\n   language families or platforms. If such content comes up unprompted, it\n   routes by shape: stack-shaped material (languages, platforms, vendors)\n   into `## Forward: tech-stack`; sequencing- or architecture-shaped\n   material (migration ideas, phasing, refactors) into\n   `## Forward: technical-roadmap` — never into sections that map onto\n   the PRD.\n\n## Entry\n\n**Three start modes**: an idea passed as the argument (adopted **verbatim**\n— no rephrasing), a path to a notes file (read fully), or nothing (one\nshort invitation, then wait).\n\n**Frame redirect**: when the input is a small problem in existing code\narriving with a ready diagnosis (an observation bundled with its claimed\ncause or fix), discovery is the wrong tool — redirect to `/101-frame`\ninstead of running the phases.\n\n**Scaffold check**: if `context/foundation/` is missing, offer to delegate\nto `/101-init` — ask, don't auto-run. The delegation goes through the\nhost's skill-invocation capability (the declared `Skill` tool); invoking\nthe sibling through a shell command is **forbidden**. After the delegate\nreturns, re-check the precondition — `context/foundation/` exists — before\nproceeding.\n\n**Resume detection comes before anything else.** If\n`context/foundation/shape-notes.md` exists: read the `checkpoint:` block,\nsummarize the state (current phase, completed phases, FR count, quality\ncheck status), then ask: resume / start over (archiving the previous file\nto `context/foundation/archive/`) / cancel. On resume, **summarize each\ncompleted phase in 1–2 sentences — never replay it**; replaying frustrates\nthe user and risks contradicting decisions they already made.\n\n## Context-type detection (skip entirely on resume — the type is locked)\n\nThree-tier scoring, not a single indicator:\n- **strong signals**: version-control history with real commits; a\n  dependency lock file (proof of an actual dependency resolution);\n- **weak signal**: a manifest alone (a freshly initialized directory has\n  one too);\n- **corroborating signals**: source directories, framework configs, CI\n  definitions.\n\nA fresh empty directory must never be classified as an existing system; a\nlone manifest yields a detection **with the ambiguity stated out loud**,\nnot a confident verdict. The result is confirmed by the user and written\ninto the frontmatter immediately (`context_type` steers routing in\n`/101-prd`).\n\nShell note: write the detection commands separately for POSIX and\nPowerShell and never machine-translate one into the other — a\nvariable-in-loop interpolation pattern that is fine in POSIX reads as a\ndrive designation in PowerShell and triggers a permission prompt for a\nnonexistent resource.\n\n## The discovery loop (identical in every phase)\n\n1. Open the phase with one sentence about what it produces plus **one open\n   question** — the user attempts the first pass themselves.\n2. If the answer leaves ambiguities, surface 3–5 **gray areas** as choices.\n   Every option is a real position with a tradeoff — no fillers (G-06).\n3. Exactly one option is marked recommended and listed first; always\n   include \"I don't know yet\".\n4. **Reflect the decision back in one sentence** for confirmation before\n   writing anything to disk.\n5. Write the section and bump the `checkpoint:` block (including\n   `gray_areas_resolved` topic → decision pairs).\n\nUse the host's question tool if one exists (discover it by shape, tell the\nuser which you picked; degrade to numbered plain text otherwise — G-09).\nSpeak the user's language throughout (G-11); keep internal phase numbers\nand field names out of the conversation (G-10).\n\n## Phases (6 content + quality check + closing)\n\n**1. Vision.** Split the answer into four components — the pain, the\nperson, the moment, today's cost — and reflect each SEPARATELY. A vague\ncomponent (\"everyone\", \"always\") triggers one Socratic question: e.g. what\nwould have to be true for this to be the wrong problem to solve, or who\nspecifically did you see with this problem in the last month.\nBrownfield adds a fifth component: **what must be preserved** — helper\nquestion: what would page the on-call if this change broke it. The\nbrownfield current-state description lands in a `## Current State` body\nsection — it maps to the PRD's `## Current State Overview`, and its\nheading is the third conjunct of `/101-prd`'s brownfield scoring signal.\n\n**2. Access.** Short: how the person reaches the product. Offer the\nconcrete access shapes as the option set (G-06 rules apply — one\nrecommended and listed first, a tradeoff on every option, always a\nfree-text exit): credential-based sign-in; delegated/third-party sign-in;\npasswordless (magic link/OTP); device-local profile with no server\nidentity; shareable access token/link; explicit single-user/no-separation.\nAsk about role separation only if access exists at all. Brownfield:\ndescribe current state first, then two closed questions about whether it\nchanges.\n\n**3. Scope discipline.** The user describes the smallest complete flow\nproving the product works; reflect it back as a numbered sequence and ask\nabout feasibility within a short horizon. Brownfield: reflect the delta\nitself as a numbered sequence, and ask one **blast-radius** question —\nwhat else touches the area being changed. Compatibility limits and\nconstraints surfaced along the way (blast radius, things the delta must\nnot break) land in a `## Constraints & Compatibility` body section — it\nmaps to the PRD section of the same name.\nThe budget key is context-typed: `mvp_weeks` (greenfield) /\n`delivery_weeks` (brownfield). This phase — and the frontmatter skeleton —\nwrites the key matching `context_type` and reads back the same key it\nwrote; a legacy file carrying either key is accepted on read (see the\ncontract).\n**Scope-cost disclosure** fires on ANY of: more than ~6 distinct user\nactions before first value; declared work exceeding the declared\n`timeline_budget` (~3 weeks is the anchor when no budget is set yet);\nseveral integrations required before any payoff. Name the\nanti-pattern (**MVP-too-big**), list the expensive pieces by name, and\noffer three exits (G-19): **narrow** (with concrete scope-down moves) /\n**consciously accept the longer horizon** / **redraw the flow**.\nAccepting the longer horizon writes a dated confirmation into the notes\n(the date — like the archive filename on a start-over — per the shared\ndate-acquisition rule: `date -u +%F` via the shell, never recalled;\ncontract: `../101-new/references/change-file-format.md`) — and from then\non the skill stops nagging. A confirmation is a gate; repeated warnings\nare not.\n\n**4. Functional requirements.** One-line canonical shape, three-digit\nnumbers, two-value priority (see the contract). Group thematically above\n~5 items. At least the main path becomes a user story with the three-part\nGiven/When/Then skeleton. Brownfield adds the change category per item:\nnew / changed / **explicitly preserved** (the third is a defensive\nrequirement — preserving something becomes a decision, not an assumption).\n\n**Socratic round (separate, batched, after the FR phase).** Hard rule:\n**exactly one challenge per requirement — no more, no fewer.** The question:\nwhat would have to be true for delivering this requirement to HURT the\nproduct — or what is the strongest counter-argument. Answer options come\nfrom the requirement's domain, never generic; the \"no counter-argument\"\noption goes **last**, so the user weighs the objection first. The outcome\nlands as the Socratic block-quote under the FR. Revising a requirement is\nallowed and updates `frs_drafted`. Above ~15 FRs the skill may **propose**\nnarrowing the round to must-haves only — soft-gate style: the user\nconfirms, and the confirmation is recorded in the checkpoint as a\n`socratic_scope` entry under `gray_areas_resolved`. Without that recorded\nconfirmation, every FR gets its challenge — never a silent reduction.\n\n**5. Business rule.** Ask for **one sentence** that distinguishes this\nproduct from a generic record list.\n**Empty-CRUD detection**: if the answer reduces to adding, viewing,\nediting and deleting records with no decision made by the application,\nname the anti-pattern (**empty-CRUD**) and offer the list of real rule\nshapes: recommendation, prioritization, classification, validation,\nscoring, state workflow, calculation. The user picks a shape and retries\nthe one-sentence question — or consciously accepts the label, which writes\na **blocking** gap entry. Never invent a rule; never infer one from nouns\n(G-13).\nBrownfield: classify as new rule / modification of an existing rule\n(describe both states) / purely infrastructural change (empty-CRUD check\ndoes not apply).\n**NFRs by reformulation**: when the user states a property mechanically\n(\"add a cache\"), reflect it back as an externally observable property\n(\"a returning visitor sees the page within X s\") BEFORE writing — this is\nthe moment to fix it; later the generator can only reject it.\n**Never ask about data objects.** Nouns emerge from FRs and stories;\nasking for fields and entities is forbidden — a field-level blocker goes\nto open questions instead.\n\n**6. Product framing.** Three short questions asked ONE AT A TIME, in\nplain language, no field names shown: what are we building — anchored by\nhow users reach the product today (browser, phone, terminal, another\nsystem) (`product_type`); audience scale (`target_scale`) — map the\nanswer onto the numeric bands in the contract's band table (`small` up to\n~100 users, `medium` ~100 to ~10 000, `large` above ~10 000), never guess\nthe value; time\nconstraints (`timeline_budget`, under the context-typed key above) —\ncapture a hard deadline when one exists\n(`hard_deadline`) and whether the work happens only after hours\n(`after_hours_only`). After the scale question, one probe: how would the\nbusiness rule change at 100× the scale. Close with one multi-select round\non **excluded scope**, options drawn from the user's domain (3–5).\nTechnology exclusions do NOT go here — the forward blocks.\n\n**7. Quality check (soft gate, G-03).** Check the required elements\n(vision, persona, ≥1 FR in canonical shape, ≥1 story, one-sentence rule,\naccess control, non-goals, timeline acknowledgment; brownfield: at least\none explicitly-preserved item whenever the change touches existing\nbehavior; plus a project-artifacts check — the notes reference the real\nproject artifacts/paths wherever the conversation mentioned them), print\nthe table, name **every gap with a one-line consequence**, then offer:\nfill now / accept and finish / return to a named phase. Acceptance sets\n`quality_check_status: gaps-accepted` (clean pass → `clean`) and appends\nthe gap list to a `## Quality cross-check` section — `/101-prd` mirrors\nthose into Open Questions.\nThe gate must **NOT** check for testing-strategy, deployment/CI, or\nimplementation-decision content — those are not schema sections, and hard\nrule 2 forbids collecting them; volunteered content of those kinds\nalready routes to the forward blocks.\n\n**8. Closing.** Final write, validation against the section contract\n(G-04), summary table and ONE next command (G-20): `/101-prd`.\n\n## Invariants\n\n- Facilitator, not author; zero stack pre-decisions.\n- Anti-patterns are named by name with concrete exits (G-19) — a vague\n  \"your idea has problems\" invalidates the gate.\n- The gate is soft: the user may override ANY gap; refusing to finish is\n  outside this skill's authority.\n- Resume summarizes, never replays.\n\n## Thresholds\n\n- Content phases: 6 (+ quality check + closing).\n- Gray areas per phase: 3–5. Excluded-scope options: 3–5.\n- Socratic challenges: exactly 1 per FR. Above ~15 FRs, must-haves-only is\n  allowed solely via a confirmed override recorded in the checkpoint.\n- Scope-cost triggers: >~6 actions before value; declared work over the\n  `timeline_budget` (default anchor ~3 weeks); several integrations before\n  payoff.\n\n## Touchpoints\n\n- Writes `context/foundation/shape-notes.md` (checkpoint + content).\n- Content ordered to map section-to-section onto the PRD contract.\n- Brownfield notes additionally carry `## Current State` (→ the PRD's\n  `## Current State Overview`) and `## Constraints & Compatibility`\n  (→ the PRD section of the same name).\n- `## Forward: tech-stack` carries stack-shaped content for\n  `/101-tech-stack-selector` and `/101-infra-research`;\n  `## Forward: technical-roadmap` carries sequencing/architecture content\n  (migration ideas, phasing, refactors) for `/101-roadmap`. Both sit\n  outside the PRD schema; `/101-prd` reprints or summarizes both in its\n  accounting.\n- May read a prior `/101-opportunity-map` or `/101-mom-test` artifact as\n  input material (decisions already made there are not re-asked — G-07).\n\n\nARGUMENTS: aplikacja webowa oceniająca kotki. dwa kotki, jeden po lewej, jeden po prawej. kliknięcie jednego to preferencja (+1). po kliknięciu wyświetlają się dwa kolejne kotki. po 10 rundach wyniki (globalne hall of cat fame).\nassistant: \nBash({\"command\":\"ls -la /course/.claude/skills/101-shape/ /course/.claude/skills/101-shape/references/ 2>/dev/null; echo \\\"---SHAPE NOTES---\\\"; ls -la /course/context/foundation/ 2>/dev/null\",\"description\":\"Check skill references and existing shape-notes\"})\nRead({\"file_path\":\"/course/.claude/skills/101-shape/references/prd-schema.md\"})\nuser: /course/.claude/skills/101-shape/:\ntotal 28\ndrwxr-xr-x  3 root root  4096 Aug 28 11:40 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 32 root root  4096 Aug 28 11:40 ..\n-rw-r--r--  1 root root 14052 Aug 28 11:40 SKILL.md\ndrwxr-xr-x  2 root root  4096 Aug 28 11:40 references\n\n/course/.claude/skills/101-shape/references/:\ntotal 24\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 root root  4096 Aug 28 11:40 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 3 root root  4096 Aug 28 11:40 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 12764 Aug 28 11:40 prd-schema.md\n---SHAPE NOTES---\ntotal 12\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 root root 4096 Aug 28 11:54 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 5 root root 4096 Aug 28 11:54 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 root root 1379 Aug 28 11:54 README.md\n1\t# Contract: shape-notes checkpoint + PRD schema\n2\t\n3\tOne document, three consumers. Owner of the shape-notes format: 101-shape.\n4\tOwner of the PRD format: 101-prd (generator). Read by 101-roadmap,\n5\t101-tech-stack-selector, 101-stack-assess. Section names and ORDER are the\n6\tcontract — downstream tools split these documents on H2 headings.\n7\t\n8\t---\n9\t\n10\t## Part 1 — Shape-notes (`context/foundation/shape-notes.md`)\n11\t\n12\t### Frontmatter\n13\t\n14\t```yaml\n15\t---\n16\tproject: \"<name>\"\n17\tcontext_type: greenfield | brownfield\n18\tcreated: YYYY-MM-DD\n19\tupdated: YYYY-MM-DD\n20\tproduct_type: web-app | api | cli | library | mobile-app | automation\n21\ttarget_scale:\n22\t  users: small | medium | large\n23\t  qps: low | medium | high\n24\t  data_volume: small | medium | large\n25\ttimeline_budget:\n26\t  mvp_weeks: <int>\n27\t  hard_deadline: <YYYY-MM-DD or null>\n28\t  after_hours_only: true | false\n29\tcheckpoint:\n30\t  current_phase: <int>              # 1..6 content phases, 7 = quality check, 8 = final\n31\t  phases_completed: [<ints>]\n32\t  frs_drafted: <int>\n33\t  quality_check_status: not-run | clean | gaps-accepted\n34\t  gray_areas_resolved:\n35\t    - topic: \"<short label>\"\n36\t      decision: \"<one or two sentences>\"\n37\t---\n38\t```\n39\t\n40\tCompatibility (T-04): legacy `quality_check_status` values map on read:\n41\t`pending` → `not-run`, `warned` → `gaps-accepted`, `accepted` → `clean`.\n42\t101 emits only the 101 values.\n43\t\n44\t### Resume behavior (driven by the checkpoint)\n45\t\n46\tOn re-entry: summarize each completed phase in 1–2 sentences (never replay\n47\tit), enter the first unfinished phase. Offer: resume / start over (archiving\n48\tthe previous version) / cancel. `context_type` is locked from the previous\n49\tsession — do not re-detect.\n50\t\n51\t### Body\n52\t\n53\tContent sections are laid out in **the same order as the PRD sections\n54\tbelow**, so the generator maps section-to-section. Two extras:\n55\t\n56\t- `## Forward: tech-stack` — stack-shaped content that surfaced during\n57\t  shaping (deployment targets, locked vendors, language hints). Explicitly\n58\t  OUTSIDE the PRD schema; consumed by 101-tech-stack-selector and\n59\t  101-infra-research.\n60\t- `## Forward: technical-roadmap` — sequencing/architecture-shaped content\n61\t  volunteered during shaping (migration ideas, phasing, refactors). Also\n62\t  outside the PRD schema; consumed by 101-roadmap and 101-plan as input\n63\t  material. 101-prd reprints both forward blocks verbatim in its closing\n64\t  accounting (R-101-29/33).\n65\t- `## Quality cross-check` — written by the closing soft gate: either \"all\n66\t  elements present, no gaps recorded\" or the named list of accepted gaps\n67\t  (which 101-prd mirrors into `## Open Questions`).\n68\t\n69\tBrownfield-only body sections (R-101-156) — named so downstream mechanisms\n70\tcan bind to them:\n71\t\n72\t- `## Current State` — what exists today; MAPS TO the PRD's\n73\t  `## Current State Overview`; its heading is the third conjunct of\n74\t  101-prd's brownfield scoring signal #1.\n75\t- `## Constraints & Compatibility` — limits the delta must respect; maps\n76\t  to the PRD section of the same name.\n77\t\n78\tFunctional requirements in shape-notes already use the canonical FR shape\n79\t(below) and carry Socratic block-quotes:\n80\t\n81\t```markdown\n82\t- FR-010: A reservation must be paid in full before it is confirmed. Priority: must-have\n83\t  > Socratic: Counter-argument considered: \"…\". Resolution: …\n84\t```\n85\t\n86\tor `> Socratic: No counter-argument raised; stands as written.`\n87\t\n88\t---\n89\t\n90\t## Part 2 — PRD (`context/foundation/prd.md`)\n91\t\n92\t### Frontmatter\n93\t\n94\t```yaml\n95\t---\n96\tproject: \"<name>\"\n97\tversion: <int>\n98\tstatus: draft | final\n99\tcreated: YYYY-MM-DD\n100\tcontext_type: greenfield | brownfield\n101\tproduct_type: <as above>\n102\ttarget_scale: { users, qps, data_volume }\n103\ttimeline_budget: { mvp_weeks, hard_deadline, after_hours_only }\n104\t---\n105\t```\n106\t\n107\tThe header carries **product priors only**. Team size, language, runtime,\n108\tplatform are deliberately absent — they belong to the tech-stack step. The\n109\tonly stack-shaped intent a PRD may carry is `product_type` (plus whatever\n110\tthe shape-notes forward block records, outside this file).\n111\t\n112\t### Greenfield variant — H2 sections, this exact order and spelling\n113\t\n114\t1. `## Vision & Problem Statement`\n115\t2. `## User & Persona`\n116\t3. `## Success Criteria` — required subsections `### Primary`,\n117\t   `### Secondary`, `### Guardrails`. Guardrails are things whose breakage\n118\t   is a regression even when the primary criterion is met.\n119\t4. `## User Stories` — `### US-NN: <title>`, body of **Given** / **When** /\n120\t   **Then** bullets, then `#### Acceptance Criteria` bullet list.\n121\t5. `## Functional Requirements` — one line per requirement:\n122\t   `- FR-NNN: <actor> can <capability>. Priority: must-have|nice-to-have`\n123\t   Three-digit numbering; thematic `###` groups when more than ~5 items;\n124\t   Socratic quotes carried over verbatim from shape-notes.\n125\t6. `## Non-Functional Requirements` — externally observable properties.\n126\t   A number with a unit beats a qualitative adjective; a binary property is\n127\t   also valid. Never a mechanism, a stack layer (client/server/edge/cache/\n128\t   worker), or a UI affordance.\n129\t7. `## Business Logic` — MUST open with one declarative sentence stating\n130\t   the domain rule. Supporting paragraphs describe inputs as things the\n131\t   user sees and how the user encounters the rule — never the component\n132\t   that computes it.\n133\t8. `## Access Control` — always present, even for a single-user app.\n134\t9. `## Non-Goals` — capabilities not built AND quality dimensions not\n135\t   pursued. Technology exclusions do NOT belong here (tech-stack step).\n136\t10. `## Open Questions` — mirror of every `[GAP: …]` marker (see Part 3);\n137\t    \"None.\" plus one sentence of justification when empty.\n138\t\n139\t**Hard rule: retired sections (both variants, R-101-159).** The\n140\tstructural self-review rejects a PRD containing ANY of: a data model\n141\t(entities emerge from FRs and stories; columns and relations are decided\n142\tlater), an implementation-decisions section, a testing-strategy section,\n143\tor a deployment/CI section. Input content of those kinds is routed to the\n144\tforward blocks (`## Forward: tech-stack` / `## Forward:\n145\ttechnical-roadmap`) — never silently dropped, never kept in the PRD.\n146\t\n147\t### Consumer resolution rule for versioned PRDs (T-20, R-101-151)\n148\t\n149\tEvery consumer (101-tech-stack-selector, 101-stack-assess,\n150\t101-infra-research, 101-roadmap, 101-test-plan) resolves \"the PRD\" as the\n151\t**highest-N `prd*.md`** in `context/foundation/` — `prd.md` counts as\n152\tv1 — unless an explicit path argument was given for that run. Same rule\n153\tas plans (`plan*.md`). Recorded once here; skills cite it instead of\n154\thard-coding the unversioned path.\n155\t\n156\t### Brownfield variant — H2 sections, this exact order and spelling (11 sections, T-15)\n157\t\n158\t1. `## Current State Overview` (the ONE place where naming current\n159\t   technology is legitimate)\n160\t2. `## Vision & Problem Statement`\n161\t3. `## User & Persona`\n162\t4. `## Success Criteria` (same required subsections as greenfield)\n163\t5. `## User Stories`\n164\t6. `## Change Scope` — replaces Functional Requirements; every item\n165\t   carries a category: `new` / `changed` / `removed` /\n166\t   `explicitly preserved` (the last is a defensive requirement — keeping\n167\t   behavior becomes a decision, not an assumption)\n168\t7. `## Business Logic` — delta only\n169\t8. `## Access Control` — delta only\n170\t9. `## Constraints & Compatibility`\n171\t10. `## Non-Goals`\n172\t11. `## Open Questions`\n173\t\n174\t**There is NO standalone `## Non-Functional Requirements` section in the\n175\tbrownfield variant** (matching the legacy brownfield set): quality\n176\tconstraints on the delta are recorded inside `## Constraints &\n177\tCompatibility`, phrased as externally observable properties under the\n178\tsame NFR rules. Counts for the structural self-review: greenfield 10\n179\tsections, brownfield 11.\n180\t\n181\tBrownfield content-mapping rules (R-101-158): `explicitly preserved`\n182\titems land in `## Change Scope` — NEVER in `## Non-Goals`;\n183\t`## Current State Overview` maps from the shape-notes current-state\n184\tsection; `## Constraints & Compatibility` maps from the shape-notes\n185\tconstraints section (section names: Part 1); every brownfield section is\n186\twritten as a DELTA against the current system, never as a full\n187\tre-description of it.\n188\t\n189\t### Technical-leak lint (content self-review; abort on hit)\n190\t\n191\tScan every section (except brownfield `## Current State Overview`) for:\n192\t1. vendor / hosted-service proper names,\n193\t2. schema & data-access notation (FK markers, nullability, column suffixes,\n194\t   migration/backfill vocabulary),\n195\t3. stack-layer placement (client-side, server-side, edge, cache, worker),\n196\t4. enforcement mechanism (rate-limit axis, allocation algorithm),\n197\t5. UI affordances used to state a quality property,\n198\t6. transport protocol / format,\n199\t7. implementation verbs in the Business Logic section (naming the component\n200\t   that executes the rule instead of stating the rule).\n201\t\n202\tA hit **aborts the write** and prints the phrase + category. Silent\n203\trewriting is forbidden — the user must see what leaked, to fix it at the\n204\tsource or consciously redirect it to the forward block.\n205\t\n206\t---\n207\t\n208\t## Part 3 — Gap marker\n209\t\n210\tMissing content is never invented (DESIGN G-13). In place of content:\n211\t\n212\t```\n213\t[GAP: <what is missing>]\n214\t```\n215\t\n216\tOne uniform, grepable token — `grep -c '\\[GAP:'` counts open gaps — plus a\n217\tmirrored entry in `## Open Questions`:\n218\t\n219\t```markdown\n220\t- [GAP: one-sentence domain rule] — Decide: product owner. Blocking: yes.\n221\t```\n222\t\n223\tStrictest enforcement at the Business Logic section: a missing one-sentence\n224\trule yields an empty marker and a **blocking** entry — never a stand-in\n225\trule, never a rule inferred from the nouns used in requirements.\n226\t\n227\t---\n228\t\n229\t## Legacy interop — dual-accept (T-04, R-101-08)\n230\t\n231\t101 emits the vocabulary defined above; artifacts written by the original\n232\tchain are accepted on read as follows. These aliases bind every PRD\n233\tconsumer (101-prd, 101-roadmap, 101-stack-assess):\n234\t\n235\t| Scope | 101 (emitted) | Legacy (accepted on read) |\n236\t| --- | --- | --- |\n237\t| `status` | `draft`, `final` | `reviewed`, `locked` ≡ `final` |\n238\t| `quality_check_status` | `not-run`, `clean`, `gaps-accepted` | `pending`→`not-run`, `warned`→`gaps-accepted`, `accepted`→`clean` |\n239\t| gap marker | `[GAP: …]` | `# TODO: <field> — see Open Questions` — a legacy gap MUST be detected and counted as a gap |\n240\t| brownfield H2 | `## Current State Overview` | `## Current System Overview` |\n241\t| brownfield H2 | `## Change Scope` | `## Scope of Change` |\n242\t| brownfield H2 | `## Vision & Problem Statement` | `## Problem Statement & Motivation` |\n243\t| brownfield H2 | `## Business Logic` | `## Business Logic Changes` |\n244\t| brownfield H2 | `## Access Control` | `## Access Control Changes` |\n245\t| change category | `changed` (emit) | `modified` accepted as equivalent |\n246\t| change category | `explicitly preserved` (emit) | `preserved` accepted as equivalent |\n247\t| `timeline_budget` key | `mvp_weeks` (greenfield) / `delivery_weeks` (brownfield) — context-typed emit | either key accepted on read regardless of context type; map to the emitted key for the document's context |\n248\t| `product_type` | set listed above (emit) | `desktop`, `data-pipeline`, `other` also accepted on read; new documents needing them use `other` with a one-line note — a conscious narrowing of the emitted set |\n249\t| `target_scale.*` | `small`, `medium`, `large` | `enterprise` accepted, read as `large` (an exact match — both mean the top band); the real collapse is on the LOW end, see the band table below |\n250\t\n251\t### `target_scale.users` numeric bands (T-18, R-101-147)\n252\t\n253\t| 101 value | Band | Legacy bands covered |\n254\t| --- | --- | --- |\n255\t| `small` | up to ~100 users | single digits AND ≤100 — these two legacy bands COLLAPSE into one; when the distinction matters (e.g. single-operator tools), record it in prose next to the field |\n256\t| `medium` | ~100 to ~10 000 users | ≤10 000 |\n257\t| `large` | above ~10 000 users | ≥10 000, `enterprise` |\n258\t\n259\t101-shape's audience-scale question maps free answers onto these anchors\n260\t(never guesses); 101-tech-stack-selector weights the prior against the\n261\tsame table. `target_scale.qps` and `.data_volume` keep their three-value\n262\tqualitative scale.\n263\t\n264\t### `timeline_budget` key is context-typed (T-17, R-101-146)\n265\t\n266\tGreenfield documents emit `mvp_weeks`; brownfield documents emit\n267\t`delivery_weeks` (a change's delivery estimate is not an MVP estimate).\n268\tBoth shape-notes and the PRD use the key matching their `context_type`,\n269\tand 101-shape's scope-discipline phase reads back the same key it wrote.\n270\tOn read, either key is accepted for either context (alias row above).\n271\t\n272\tH2 headings delimit sections for parsers — any consumer that splits on\n273\theadings must try the 101 name first, then the legacy alias.\n274\t\n275\t## Ownership table\n276\t\n277\t| Writer | Writes | When |\n278\t| --- | --- | --- |\n279\t| 101-shape | shape-notes (all of it, incl. checkpoint bumps) | during discovery |\n280\t| 101-prd | prd.md (whole file) | generation; regeneration via collision flow |\n281\t| 101-roadmap / 101-tech-stack-selector / 101-stack-assess | nothing | read-only |\n282\t| user | any manual edits | anytime (living document, edit-in-place) |\n283\t\nassistant: \nBash({\"command\":\"date -u +%F; echo \\\"--- markers ---\\\"; ls -a /course | grep -Ei '^(\\\\.git|package-lock\\\\.json|yarn\\\\.lock|pnpm-lock\\\\.yaml|poetry\\\\.lock|Cargo\\\\.lock|go\\\\.sum|composer\\\\.lock|Gemfile\\\\.lock|requirements\\\\.txt|package\\\\.json|pyproject\\\\.toml|Cargo\\\\.toml|go\\\\.mod|src|app|lib|tests?|\\\\.github)$' || echo \\\"brak markerów projektu\\\"\",\"description\":\"Get UTC date and detect project markers\"})\nuser: 2026-08-28\n--- markers ---\nbrak markerów projektu\nassistant: \nWrite({\"file_path\":\"/course/context/foundation/shape-notes.md\",\"content\":\"---\\nproject: \\\"Ocenianie kotków\\\"\\ncontext_type: greenfield\\ncreated: 2026-08-28\\nupdated: 2026-08-28\\nproduct_type: null\\ntarget_scale:\\n  users: null\\n  qps: null\\n  data_volume: null\\ntimeline_budget:\\n  mvp_weeks: null\\n  hard_deadline: null\\n  after_hours_only: null\\ncheckpoint:\\n  current_phase: 1\\n  phases_completed: []\\n  frs_drafted: 0\\n  quality_check_status: not-run\\n  gray_areas_resolved: []\\n---\\n\\n# Shape notes — Ocenianie kotków\\n\\n## Pomysł wyjściowy (dosłownie, słowami użytkownika)\\n\\n> aplikacja webowa oceniająca kotki. dwa kotki, jeden po lewej, jeden po\\n> prawej. kliknięcie jednego to preferencja (+1). po kliknięciu wyświetlają\\n> się dwa kolejne kotki. po 10 rundach wyniki (globalne hall of cat fame).\\n\"})\nuser: File created successfully at: /course/context/foundation/shape-notes.md (file state is current in your context — no need to Read it back)","recorded":"2026-08-28T11:56:27.566Z"}
